$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Solid phase synthesis of lacticin 3147 A2 Synthesis of ring C of lacticin
3147 A2 (32) a) General procedure for Fmoc Solid Phase Peptide Synthesis
(SPPS): The desired Fmoc amino acid (5.0 equiv to resin loading) and HOBt
(5.0 equiv) were dissolved in DMF (10 mL) and to this solution, NMM (5.0
equiv) was added, followed by PyBOP (4.9 equiv). The resulting mixture
was pre-activated for 5 min. This activated amino acid solution was then
transferred to a pre-swelled resin and reacted for 2 h. The completion of
coupling was ascertained by a negative Kaiser test and end capping was
performed by exposing the resin to 20% Ac2O in DMF for 10 min. Subsequent
Fmoc deprotection was achieved using 20% piperidine in DMF and monitored
by the absorption of the dibenzofulvene-piperidine adduct at λ = 301 nm
on a UV-Vis spectrophotometer. Loading of Fmoc-Alloc/Allyl-MeLan on
2-cholorotrityl resins A 2-cholorotrityl resin with a loading of 0.15
mmol/g was used for the synthesis. The initial loading of 0.44 mmol/g of
the resin was reduced to 0.1 mmol/g to avoid any interstrand dimerization
during the on resin-cyclization step. A solution of orthogonally
protected Fmoc-Alloc/Allyl-MeLan (107.0 mg, 0.19 mmol) and DIPEA (0.16
mL, 0.94 mmol) in dry CH2Cl2 (10 mL) was added to the 2-cholorotrityl
resin (1.25 g). The resulting mixture was shaken for 24 h and filtered.
The resin was washed with CH2Cl2 (10 mL x 3) and resuspended in DIPEA (1
mL), MeOH (1 mL) and CH2Cl2 (8 mL). The resulting mixture was bubbled
with argon gas for 1 h and filtered. The resin was then washed with
CH2Cl2 (10 mL x 3) and DMF (10 mL x 3). Deprotection of Allyl, Fmoc
groups and cyclization to form ring C on solid support Fmoc SPPS was
continued using PyBOP to couple Fmoc-Ala-OH (residue 27) and
Fmoc-Arg(Pmc)-OH (residue 26) to the amino acid on resin to give a linear
fragment on resin. A solution of Pd(PPh3)4 (58.6 mg, 0.38 mmol, 2 equiv
to resin) and PhSiH3 (0.24 mL, 0.19 mmol, 10 equiv to resin) in (1:1)
DMF/CH2Cl2 (10 mL) was reacted with the resin bound peptide, protected
from light, for 2 h. The solution was drained and the resin was washed in
the following sequence: 1) CH2Cl2 (2 x 10 mL), 2) 0.5% sodium
diethyldithiocarbamate in DMF (3 x 10 mL), 3) DMF (2 x 10 mL). The color
of the resin changed to light yellow after these washings. The Fmoc group
was removed with 20% piperidine in DMF (3 x 10 mL). The resin was washed
with CH2Cl2 (3 x 10 mL) and DMF (3 x10 mL). The cyclization to form ring
C was done by adding a solution of PyBOP (492.4 mg, 0.94 mmol, 5.0
equiv), HOBt (127.2 mg, 0.94 mmol, 5.0 equiv) and NMM (0.21 mL, 1.88
mmol, 10.0 equiv) in DMF (10 mL) to the linear peptide and reacted for 2
h. Another Fmoc-Alloc/Allyl-MeLan (3a) was coupled on resin using MeLan
(214.0 mg, 0.38 mmol, 2 equiv), HOBt (50.9 mg, 2.0 equiv), PyBOP (186.0
mg, 0.36 mmol, 1.9 equiv) and NMM (0.04 mL, 0.38 mmol, 2 equiv). A small
sample (5 mg) of resin was cleaved by shaking with 95:2.5:2.5 (TFA:
Et3SiH: H2O) for 1 h and then filtered to remove the resin. The filtrate
was concentrated in vacuo and precipitated with Et2O to give an off-white
solid. Synthesis of lacticin A2 ring B (33) 32 was elongated using Fmoc
SPPS with PyBOP to couple protected amino acids in the following order:
Fmoc-Lys(Boc)-OH, Fmoc-Thr(t-Bu)-OH. Allyl and Fmoc groups were removed
according to the procedure described above. Cyclization to form ring B
was done with PyBOP similar to ring C. The Fmoc-Pro-OH was coupled on
resin. A small portion of resin (5 mg) was cleaved and analyzed by
MALDI-TOF. Synthesis of lacticin A2 ring A (34) The residues (17-20)
required for the linear portion of ring A were introduced under standard
SPPS conditions using PyBOP in the order: 3b, Fmoc-Thr(t-Bu)-OH,
Fmoc-Asn(Trt)-OH, Fmoc-Thr(t-Bu)-OH. Removal of the Allyl and Fmoc
groups, as well as the cyclization, were performed in a similar manner to
ring C to form ring A. Then Fmoc-IIe-OH was coupled on resin. A small
sample of the resin (5 mg) was cleaved and subjected to MALDI-TOF.
Synthesis of tricyclic peptide moiety (9) Fmoc-SPPS was done using PyBOP
to couple amino acids 6-14 in the following order: Fmoc-Tyr(t-Bu)-OH,
Fmoc-Ala-OH, Fmoc-D-Ala-OH, Fmoc-Leu-OH, Fmoc-Ile-OH, Fmoc-D-Ala-OH,
Fmoc-Ile-OH, Fmoc-Ala-OH, Fmoc-Pro-OH. A small sample of the resin was
cleaved to give the tricyclic peptide 9. Synthesis of lacticin 3147 A2
(2) The Fmoc group of resin bound 9 (0.19 mmol) was removed using 20%
piperidine/DMF (3 x 5 min) and the resin was washed with NMP (3 x 10 mL)
to remove any traces of piperidine. In a separate vial, pentapeptide 10
(201.5 mg, 0.38 mmol) was dissolved in NMP (10 mL). To it, HOBt (50.9 mg,
0.38 mmol) and NMM (0.05 mL, 0.38 mmol) were added followed by PyBOP
(195.9 mg, 0.38 mmol) and reacted for 5 minutes. The preactivated
solution was then transferred to resin bound peptide and coupled for 7 h.
The completion of reaction was ascertained by MALDI-TOF MS. The resin was
cleaved with (95: 2.5: 2.5) TFA: anisole: H2O (10 mL) for 2 h. The
cleavage mixture was filtered to remove the resin and concentrated in
vacuo, followed by precipitation with cold ethyl ether and
centrifugation. lacticin A2 as a white solid. Purification of lacticin
3147 A2: The crude peptide was purified by reverse phase HPLC using a
CSC-Inertsil 150 Å/ODS-2, 5 μm 10 x 250 mm column. The crude lacticin A2
was dissolved in 24% IPA in H2O to give a concentration of 1 mg/mL. In
the method 1.0 mL injection was employed and a gradient of water and IPA
(0.1% TFA) was used to separate peptides. Gradient: Starting from 24% IPA
for 5 min and ramp up to 44% over 20 min, followed by ramping down to 24%
over 5 min, and remaining this concetration for 10 min (flow rate 2.25
mL/min, detection at 220 and 254 nm). The fraction containing the desired
product was collected as a broad peak (tR = 27.4 min), which was
concentrated in vacuo and lyophilized to give relatively pure peptide.
Second purification was done using the same condition. The fractions
containing the desired product were concentrated in vacuo and
lyophilized. lacticin A2 as a white solid (4.8 mg, 1.0%). Chiral GC/MS
Analysis of Natural Lacticin A1 and A2 Natural lantibiotics samples were
acid hydrolyzed and derivatized for analysis by chiral Gas Chromatography
Mass Spectrometry (GC/MS).1 The identity of the methyllanthionine and
lanthionine residue within each peptide was confirmed by comparison with
standard samples. Monoisotopic MW Calcd. for C126H200N33O36S3 2847.4,
found 2847.4 (M + H).
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM in the dark (in stages 4), solid-supported reaction, capping with
Ac2O/DMF, Fmoc deprotection with piperidine/DMF, 9, 14, selective
alloc/allyl deprotection in stages 4, 9, 14, reactant preactivated and
used in stages 2, 3, 7, 8, 11-13, 16-19, attachment to 2-chlorotrityl
polystyrene resin in stage 1; peptide coupling was carried out for 2
hours at room temperature; reactant from stage 1 added in stage 6
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Solid supported chemical syntheses of both components of the lantibiotic
lacticin 3147
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Liu, Wei; Chan, Alice S. H.; Liu, Hongqiang; Cochrane, Stephen A.;
Vederas, John C.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Journal of the American Chemical Society (2011), 133(36), 14216-14219

$RFMT $RIREG SCHEME20 STEP1
